        # About

        My name's Vladimir and on the Internet, I go by "allejo."

        You may recognize me as a former coworker that you liked, a mentor from a tech community, a shitposter on LinkedIn, or as a contributor to open source projects. I'm a senior software engineer currently in higher ed doing frontend development at [UC Merced](https://www.ucmerced.edu), previously I was at [Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/) until [poor company leadership laid me off in the first of many layoffs to come]({{ url(collections.posts['2023-10-21-Being-Promoted-to-Customer-at-Stack-Overflow'] }}).

        I enjoy building developer tools like documentation builders, code generators, syntax highlighters, and similar tools. Building 3D worlds is a hobby of mine so I've enjoyed contributing to BZFlag, its community, and ecosystem over the past decade.
        
        I speak at conferences and workplaces about accessibility, mentoring, frontend, and technical leadership.
        
        ## Mentoring

        Since 2019, I have mentored 30+ junior and 10+ mid-level developers. With AI on the rise, I believe now more than ever, we should be focusing on training junior and mid-level developers. Once the current generation of seniors retire or get promoted, we won't have anyone in the industry who's been learning how to fill those roles. I make it a goal to always mentor junior developers in every position I go.
        
        - While at the City of Santa Monica, I helped run the Youth Tech Program where I taught high school students how to build websites
        - While at California State University, Northridge, I started my own student development team that during my tenure, I had over 10 student hires
        - While at Stack Overflow, I volunteered as a mentor at Techtonica—a bootcamp to help women and LGBTQ+ transition into tech careers
        - While at UC Merced, I started the departments first student development team and in under 2 years, have hired 6+ students; with 2 being undocumented students I was able to work with via grants
        
        My next goal is to start lecturing at the college level for computer science, software engineering, web design, and HCI/accessibility classes.

